ope in recent years and in no small  part that has been down to the UK's  close relationship with the United  States our access in particular to US  intelligence fees which we can feed  discreetly where appropriate and with  American compliance or with American  support into Europe to bolster their  counter jihadists and other campaigns I  think is something which the European  nations in particular prize to this end  we have learnt that the heads of  Britain's three intelligence agencies  have been touring European capitals  reassuring their allies that Britain  will remain fully engaged after brexit  everyday intelligence officers from that  building mi6 share information with  their European colleagues it's normally  done on a bilateral basis but there is  also something known as the European  counterterrorism group which Britain is  a key member of and will remain so after  brexit because of the twin threats from  Islamic extremism and from Russia data  sharing within Europe is going on at  unprecedented levels and this is the  area that will be affected by brexit  whatever form that takes although not a  member of Schengen the UK is a member of  the Schengen information system giving  it access to passport data held by  fellow EU police forces in 2017 alone  that system was checked by British  police  539 million times Britain is also a  signatory of the European Arrest Warrant  it allows you members to request the  arrest and detention of criminals in  other countries without extradition  talks between them most of this  information is covered by data privacy  and data sharing rules and they're set  by the European Union now when we've  been around the table we've been able to  argue our case and the the rules on data  sharing have been very much in line with  our national security interests  after brexit we won't be in that room  and the rules will be set by other  countries and we'll have no choice but  to accept those rules with that in mind  the Prime Minister travelled to the  Munich Security Conference last year  with a proposal  in a keynote speech to global Movers &amp;  Shakers she called for a security treaty  with the EU I recognize there is no  existing security agreement between the  EU and a third country that captures the  full depth and breadth of our existing  relationship but there is precedent for  comprehensive strategic relationships  between the EU and third countries in  other fields such as trade and there is  no legal or operational reason why such  an agreement could not be reached in the  area of internal security but Sir  Richard Dearlove another former head of  mi6 has dramatically criticised Teresa  Mays current deal claiming that it puts  Britain's security in the control of the  EU the first duty of the state above  trade is the security of its citizens he  wrote in a letter the withdrawal  agreement abrogates this fundamental  contract it would place control of  aspects of our national security in  foreign hands Theresa May is called for  a security treaty relies on her getting  a deal through Parliament but even then  the UK security could still be affected  any form of brexit makes our security  more difficult to manage in the same way  that it it damages our economy the heart  of the brexit the greater the damage in  my own view is that the best outcome for  British national security is to stay in  the EU that gives us full access to all  this range of instruments the next best  is to have a negotiated arrangement as a  third country it won't be as good but  it's a lot better than nothing the worst  of all is to crash out with no deal  there there would be a really serious  and immediate problem for British  national security if in the past being  an island kept Britain safe not so  nowadays modern security is built on  cooperation alliance is the foundation  of a solid foreign policy it is if you  like about safety in numbers the cliff  edge is not a good place to be stood  Alistair bundle Sky News
 

